{"claim":"Assume identical labelled four-mode one-particle transformations may be substituted in any fixed-occupation experiment of the same duration; a localized low-mode pair source and local terminal occupation detectors already test this instance. A target rotation contact and a swap contact each have a portable real sign with vacuum fixed to +1. The numerical test gives the determinant signs (+1,-1) uniquely for these two gate types. The equivalence is an added physical premise; a localized one-particle characterization alone gives no such inference.","construction":"R_i is (c,-s;s,c)/5 on one occupant of adjacent i,i+1. S_i swaps these two modes with no one-particle sign. Z_i is -1 when mode i is occupied, including in multi-occupation configurations. Other local modes are spectators. Put A=R0 R2, B=S1 A S1. They are the rotations of the low and high binary digits of the one-particle mode index and commute. Inverse R_i is Z_i R_i Z_i, also inverse on its contact because the sign is real. The held 20-operation commutator and matched 20-operation echo have the same settings multiset and one-particle identity. Each contains eight R, four S and eight Z strokes. No feedback or intermediate reads. Return detects the difference of their lifted evolutions; signed matrices for all inputs and outputs are included.","conventions":"Bit i names occupied mode i, i=0,1,2,3. All inputs and outputs in each number sector are retained; signed_columns[input][output] is an exact rational amplitude, whose square is its complete unconditional port weight. Independent exterior-minor evaluations check the determinant lift across all five sectors. Gate timings can be matched with identities, which are assumed to preserve all occupations. Identifying a response only up to a phase common to its whole fixed-number sector is sufficient for this detector comparison.","qualifications":"The commutator here tests portable real signs for these gates, orientations and bond locations, with the single-site sign instrument independently calibrated and stable on spectators. For a general unit-modulus contact g, Z R Z retains g on the doubly occupied state whereas the true gate inverse has conjugate g. The signed inverse protocol therefore does not prove the general complex-phase result; that case requires a forward-word or separately calibrated true-inverse test. Compiler-dependent contacts, hidden memories, and phases common to a complete fixed-number circuit remain distinct possibilities. The raw AB/BA probabilities can coincide with incorrect target sign; downstream probes or the closed loop reveal it. The reversed rational numerator (4,3) is an additional tested setting.","rational_conjugacy_obstruction":[{"reflection_numerators":[[3,4],[4,-3]],"denominator":5,"rational_axis":[2,1],"squared_norm":5,"normalized_rational_axis_vector_exists":false},{"reflection_numerators":[[4,3],[3,-4]],"denominator":5,"rational_axis":[3,1],"squared_norm":10,"normalized_rational_axis_vector_exists":false}],"rational_conjugacy_scope":"Trying only to conjugate the fixed calibrated coordinate reflection Z into the reflection RZ inside O(2,Q) fails for these two angles: their +1 axes squared norms are 5 and 10, respectively. A rational nonzero multiple cannot normalize either. The quarter-turn (sine=1) and zero-angle are distinct degenerate controls; uniqueness above is asserted for the two tabulated angles. The four-mode commutator uses the target itself rather than an uncalibrated generator at half its angle.","cases":[{"cos_numerator":3,"sin_numerator":4,"one_mode_denominator":5,"programs":{"AB":["R0","R2","S1","R0","R2","S1"],"BA":["S1","R0","R2","S1","R0","R2"],"AB_read":["R0","R2","S1","R0","R2","S1","R0","R1"],"BA_read":["S1","R0","R2","S1","R0","R2","R0","R1"],"commutator":["R0","R2","S1","R0","R2","S1","Z2","R2","Z2","Z0","R0","Z0","S1","Z2","R2","Z2","Z0","R0","Z0","S1"],"matched_echo":["R0","R2","S1","R0","R2","S1","S1","Z2","R2","Z2","Z0","R0","Z0","S1","Z2","R2","Z2